  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1213 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nAs a shortwave trough enters from the Pacific, the upper-level ridge \ncurrently situated largely over the Southwestern US appears to break \ndown and shift east early in the work week. As the ridge's lifecycle \nprogresses, it will continue providing northwesterly flow aloft with \nnegligible precipitation chances and a warming trend. High \ntemperatures across the Panhandles are expected to be in the mid 80s \nby Tuesday. \n\nBy the middle of the week, the shortwave trough will deepen and \ntrack into New Mexico and Texas as a positively tilted trough. The \nupper-level trough and associated mid-level low's trajectory appears \nto stay west and south of the Panhandles through the rest of the \nwork week. This trend will keep our precipitation chances very low \nthrough the remainder of the week. \n\nModel guidance continues to hint at a trough dipping into the \nWestern US this upcoming weekend, though there is a wide range of \nsolutions depicting how the trough will evolve. It will impact our \nweather in some form, though its exact impact is still to be \ndetermined. \n\nAbdullayev/Vanden Bosch",
